WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:04.021
- [Voiceover] Fireworks are
associated with celebration,

00:00:04.021 --> 00:00:08.188
but they originally stem from
a place of fear and folklore.

00:00:11.967 --> 00:00:14.722
In 200 BC, some people in China believed

00:00:14.722 --> 00:00:17.913
that a tribe of scary
Bigfoot-esque mountain men

00:00:17.913 --> 00:00:19.721
lurked in the foothills.

00:00:19.721 --> 00:00:22.293
And the most logical way
to ward off this enemy?

00:00:22.293 --> 00:00:23.663
Scare ‘em.

00:00:23.663 --> 00:00:25.197
They realized that heating bamboo shoots

00:00:25.197 --> 00:00:26.982
caused air pockets in the plant to burst,

00:00:26.982 --> 00:00:28.734
creating a loud popping noise

00:00:28.734 --> 00:00:31.886
which they thought would
keep the mountain men away.

00:00:31.886 --> 00:00:34.310
And it allegedly worked!

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:37.222
800 years later, Chinese muggles set out

00:00:37.222 --> 00:00:39.133
on a quest for immortality,

00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:41.392
and while they didn't
find the elixir of life,

00:00:41.392 --> 00:00:42.790
these alchemists ended up creating

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:45.246
the world's first batch of gunpowder.

00:00:45.246 --> 00:00:47.038
Funny how the desire to live forever

00:00:47.038 --> 00:00:49.478
was essentially a gateway
drug to modern warfare.

00:00:49.478 --> 00:00:52.458
But, anyway, back to the story.

00:00:52.458 --> 00:00:54.707
They took this new fire
drug, as they called it,

00:00:54.707 --> 00:00:57.817
stuffed it into their
bamboo shoots, and kaboom!

00:00:57.817 --> 00:00:59.282
Fireworks were born.

00:00:59.282 --> 00:01:01.002
Excitement over the invention of gunpowder

00:01:01.002 --> 00:01:05.162
and fiery sticks eventually
made its way around the world.

00:01:05.162 --> 00:01:08.586
Fast forward to July
3rd, 1776, the day before

00:01:08.586 --> 00:01:11.074
the Declaration of
Independence was signed.

00:01:11.074 --> 00:01:12.674
John Adams wrote a letter to his wife

00:01:12.674 --> 00:01:14.961
with big plans for commemorating the day.

00:01:14.961 --> 00:01:16.402
- [Voiceover] It ought to be solemnized

00:01:16.402 --> 00:01:20.638
with pomp and parade...
bonfires and illuminations

00:01:20.638 --> 00:01:23.555
from this time forward forevermore.

00:01:24.526 --> 00:01:27.181
- [Voiceover] Adams wanted
illuminations, he got em.

00:01:27.181 --> 00:01:29.501
The sky lit up with fireworks on July 4th,

00:01:29.501 --> 00:01:30.990
and from that day forward fireworks

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:34.309
have become a ubiquitous
American celebratory tradition.

00:01:34.309 --> 00:01:36.246
So I guess the next time
you see the sky ablaze,

00:01:36.246 --> 00:01:37.566
you can thank Bigfoot.

00:01:37.566 --> 00:01:38.702
I mean, the Chinese.

00:01:38.702 --> 00:01:39.814
Or John Adams.

00:01:39.814 --> 00:01:43.981
Your call.
(patriotic music playing)

